# Orchestrate Skill Set — Key Points

3-tier autonomous orchestration. User hands mission to L1. Everything below runs headless.

## Topology

```
User
 └─ L1 (slay-mission) — root slay task
     └─ L2 (slay-exec) — sub-task, own PTY
         ├─ L3 (slay-impl) — sub-sub-task, own PTY
         ├─ L3 (slay-impl) — sub-sub-task, own PTY
         └─ L3 (slay-commit) — sub-sub-task, own PTY
```

Hierarchy: L1 → L2 → L3. Strict. No skipping levels.

All inter-agent comm = **PTY message + submit**. Sole channel. No shared files, no locks, no task comments, no asset handoff.

---

## L1 — slay-mission

**Role.** Mission lead. Keeps op on track.

**Tempo.** Continuous observer, default silent. Reads every L2 report. Sends PTY msg down ONLY when:
- Drift detected — L2 diverging from mission intent
- Concrete tip materially improves outcome
- L2 escalates up (direct reply expected)

"Default silent" = speaks only when adds value. Silence is the norm, not exception.

**Does.**
- Receive free-format mission from user
- Translate → big picture + scoped mission for L2
- Spawn exactly one L2 sub-task (`slay task new`)
- Send initial PTY msg + submit (format below)
- Observe L2 progress reports
- Send course-correction PTY msg down only when gate passes
- Final review on L2 "done" signal
- Mark root task done after approval

**Does NOT.**
- Spawn L3s
- Touch code
- Commit
- Micromanage L2's plan

**Intervention gate (before sending msg).**
- L2 off-track, or just different-but-valid? Uncertain → silent
- Tip change outcome materially? No → silent

---

## L2 — slay-exec

**Role.** Executor. Coordinates L3s.

**Does.**
- Receive mission + big picture from L1
- Decompose into independent work units
- Decide parallelism: parallel iff units independent (no file overlap, no logical dep)
- Spawn L3s via `slay task new`, one per unit
- Send initial PTY msg + submit per L3 (format below)
- Monitor L3 replies
- Handle L3 escalations — resolve at L2 or escalate to L1
- Spawn `slay-commit` agents serially, near end of impl phase
- Report progress + completion to L1

**Parallelism rules.**
- Parallel only if L3 scopes don't overlap (logical + file-level)
- Overlap → sequence them
- slay-commit agents always serial — never parallel

**Commit orchestration.**
- L3 impl agents never commit. Only `slay-commit` agents do
- L2 waits for impl L3s to finish (or reach stable state) before spawning commit agents
- L2 spawns commit agents serially: one at a time, wait for done, spawn next
- Each commit agent gets: scope (files/lines), commit msg guidance

**Escalation handling.**
- L3 escalates → L2 decides: in-scope (replan/reassign/fix) or escalate to L1

---

## L3 — slay-impl

**Role.** Execute one scoped impl mission. Never commits.

**Does.**
- Receive scope + big picture + chain position from L2
- Execute scoped mission, stay in bounds
- Report outcome to L2 via PTY msg + submit
- Escalate to L2 when: blocker outside scope, mission unclear, conflict found

**Hard rules.**
- **NEVER commit to git.** Absolute.
- Never spawn sub-tasks
- Never decide own scope
- Never talk to L1 directly

**Escalation criteria.**
- "Inside my specific mission?" Yes → handle. No → escalate
- Uncertain → escalate (cheap)

---

## L3 — slay-commit

**Role.** Commit one scoped diff. Never implements.

**Does.**
- Receive commit scope + msg guidance from L2
- Stage specified files/hunks
- Verify diff matches scope
- Commit with guided msg (Conventional Commits)
- Report commit hash to L2 via PTY msg + submit
- Escalate to L2 on: conflict, dirty state outside scope, scope unclear

**Hard rules.**
- **ONLY commit work.** No edits to impl code beyond stage/commit
- Never spawn sub-tasks
- Never talk to L1 directly
- One commit per spawn (L2 respawns for next)

Separate skill from `slay-impl` because hard rules are inverse. Prevents prompt branching.

